Note what I said in the OP:

  3. Facing

I know, I know, it's impossible/insanely hard to implement facing into 
NetHack. But it's not impossible to implement it in the interface. Make 
monsters and the player be generated facing forward (towards the 
screen), then face the direction they last moved in. Monsters fleeing 
would always face away from the player, or we could get YAFMs where 
rogues get the message "You strike [slow monster] from behind" even 
though [slow monster] is facing them. This would create a use for the 
arrow keys in controlling movement - left and right would change the 
direction you're facing (without using any time, of course), and up and 
down would move in/away from the direction you're facing. This could be 
changed back to its default behavior, of course. An arrow would point in 
the direction the playr is facing, so someone polymorphed into, say, a 
lichen, wouldn't have to wonder where it was facing (it could happen, 
like from a polymorph trap).

